The Maine State House is a good fit for a **short, low-key stop** rather than a major sightseeing detour. Completed in 1832 and built of Maine granite, it has a comparatively restrained appearance. One traveler on the Bangor-to-Portland drive describes its dome as less ornate than those at other statehouses; they took a quick self-guided look and continued on. [No Home Just Roam](w1)

So, if you’re hoping for a lavish interior or a dramatic, highly decorated dome, you may find it plain. But if you enjoy seeing historic civic buildings—and you’re already passing through Augusta—it seems worth a brief visit. The listed address is **210 State Street**; the places listing gives weekday hours of **8 a.m.–5 p.m.** Since access can depend on building operations, check the legislature’s site before making plans. [Wandering Jana](w2) also found it plain and small, but said the visit didn’t take long.

{"map": [{"ref": "W1196631395"}]}

